course: dictionary
lesson: 1321
title: Using a calculator wisely
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A calculator is a small electronic machine that does sums and other maths quickly. It can also be a computer program or a phone app that does the same job. The word is a noun, so it names a thing you can hold or open.
explain: Maths lessons use calculators for big or hard sums and for checking answers. «Use a calculator to check» means you try the sum first, then use the machine to see if you got it right.
explain: You still need to learn mental and written methods. «Learn a skill first, then use tools to check it, the way you use a calculator.» Some tests do not allow calculators, so always read the rules.
explain: Make a sensible estimate before you press any keys. If you think 19 × 5 is about 100 but the screen shows 950, you have probably pressed a wrong key.
fact: A calculator is a small electronic machine for doing sums and other maths quickly.
fact: A calculator can also be a computer program or a phone app.
fact: «Calculator allowed» means you may use a calculator for that task.
fact: Some calculators have a small solar panel that keeps working as long as there is light.
fact: A quick estimate helps you spot a wrong key press.
exercise: What is a calculator? | A long ruler for measuring lines | *A small electronic machine for doing sums quickly | A rubber for removing mistakes
exercise: Ava estimates 19 × 5 is about 100, but her calculator shows 950. What is the most likely reason? | Estimates are never useful | The calculator is always right, so 950 must be correct | *She has probably pressed a wrong key | Multiplying always makes numbers smaller
exercise: «Some chatbots guess numbers like words, so big sums can come out wrong; check with a calculator.» Why does the sentence say to check? | *Because some chatbots can get big sums wrong | Because chatbots are always right | Because calculators can spell words | Because big sums are never needed
test: On a test sheet, «calculator allowed» means what? | *You may use a calculator for that task | You must draw a picture of a calculator | You need to buy a calculator before the test | You must not use a calculator for any question
test: Which is a good first step before using a calculator on a hard sum? | Ask the calculator to guess the answer | *Make a sensible estimate | Press random keys until something appears | Turn off the lights
exam: Which statement is true? | A calculator cannot do multiplication | *You should still learn mental and written methods, and you can use a calculator to check your answers | Calculators are never allowed in any maths test | Calculators can only be used by adults
